WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.886
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.186 --> 00:00:08.294
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.594 --> 00:00:14.845
Unit ten: Venture Capital. Today you will describe investment steps with before + gerund clauses.

4
00:00:15.145 --> 00:00:17.646
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.902
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.007
Venture capital.

7
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:30.522
Venture capital means money invested in young, high-growth companies in exchange for ownership.

8
00:00:31.117 --> 00:00:33.894
Venture capital funded the expansion to Vietnam.

9
00:00:34.483 --> 00:00:37.693
Your turn. Say: Venture capital.

10
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:41.743
Venture capital.

11
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:43.426
Equity.

12
00:00:44.417 --> 00:00:47.717
Equity means ownership in a company, usually as shares.

13
00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:51.605
The founders gave up 20% equity for the investment.

14
00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:54.683
Your turn. Say: Equity.

15
00:00:57.533 --> 00:00:58.465
Equity.

16
00:00:59.217 --> 00:01:00.218
Term sheet.

17
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:05.062
Term sheet means a document setting out the main conditions of an investment.

18
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.346
The term sheet included a seat on the board.

19
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.498
Your turn. Say: Term sheet.

20
00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:15.532
Term sheet.

21
00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:17.401
Due diligence.

22
00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:22.425
Due diligence means a detailed investigation before a deal is completed.

23
00:01:23.017 --> 00:01:26.015
Due diligence uncovered unpaid taxes.

24
00:01:26.617 --> 00:01:29.803
Your turn. Say: Due diligence.

25
00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:34.039
Due diligence.

26
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:35.854
Valuation.

27
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:40.777
Valuation means the agreed value of a company at the time of investment.

28
00:01:41.383 --> 00:01:45.090
A higher valuation means founders give away less of the company.

29
00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:48.429
Your turn. Say: Valuation.

30
00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:52.488
Valuation.

31
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.319
Exit strategy.

32
00:01:56.317 --> 00:02:00.731
Exit strategy means a plan for how investors will eventually sell their stake.

33
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:04.558
Their exit strategy is acquisition by a regional bank.

34
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.696
Your turn. Say: Exit strategy.

35
00:02:11.533 --> 00:02:12.918
Exit strategy.

36
00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:14.824
Portfolio.

37
00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:19.267
Portfolio means the collection of companies an investor holds.

38
00:02:19.883 --> 00:02:23.392
Only two firms in the portfolio became highly profitable.

39
00:02:23.983 --> 00:02:26.868
Your turn. Say: Portfolio.

40
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:31.803
Portfolio.

41
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.062
Return on investment.

42
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:39.325
Return on investment means the profit gained relative to the amount invested.

43
00:02:39.917 --> 00:02:43.553
One success can deliver a return on investment of fifty times.

44
00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:47.011
Your turn. Say: Return on investment.

45
00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:51.367
Return on investment.

46
00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:55.632
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.483 --> 00:03:02.886
Visal, a Singapore fund almost invested in a Phnom Penh logistics start-up, then walked away. Why?

48
00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:12.879
Before signing the term sheet, they conducted due diligence and found the delivery numbers had been exaggerated.

49
00:03:13.983 --> 00:03:16.749
Couldn't they have simply negotiated a lower valuation?

50
00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:28.444
The problem was trust. Before committing funds, investors need to believe the founders report honestly, because they'll be partners for years.

51
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.503
So what should the founders have done?

52
00:03:32.617 --> 00:03:41.178
Shared realistic figures from the start. Before pitching, every founder should check that the data can survive close inspection.

53
00:03:42.333 --> 00:03:44.205
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:48.164
Grammar focus: Before plus gerund clauses.

55
00:03:48.464 --> 00:03:49.697
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.383 --> 00:03:53.741
Investors conduct due diligence before committing funds.

57
00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.915
Founders should verify their data before pitching to investors.

58
00:03:58.817 --> 00:04:02.024
The fund reviewed the contracts before signing the term sheet.

59
00:04:02.967 --> 00:04:14.057
When the subject of both actions is the same, use 'before / after + -ing' for a concise, formal style: Before investing, we… (= Before we invest, we…). Never 'before to invest'.

60
00:04:14.817 --> 00:04:18.827
Be careful! Don't say: Investors check before to commit.

61
00:04:20.767 --> 00:04:23.567
Say: Investors check before committing.

62
00:04:23.867 --> 00:04:24.950
Before + -ing.

63
00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:28.270
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:29.067 --> 00:04:31.008
High Risk, High Reward.

65
00:04:31.308 --> 00:04:37.325
Venture capital funds invest in young companies that could grow very quickly, accepting that most will fail.

66
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:45.592
Before investing, a fund conducts due diligence, examining finances, contracts, technology and the founders' integrity.

67
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:54.532
In return for capital, investors receive equity, so the valuation agreed in the term sheet determines how much of the company the founders keep.

68
00:04:55.533 --> 00:05:02.630
Because one exceptional success can repay many losses, funds build diversified portfolios rather than betting on a single firm.

69
00:05:03.633 --> 00:05:10.660
Before accepting venture money, founders should consider whether rapid growth and an eventual exit truly match their own goals.

70
00:05:11.767 --> 00:05:15.606
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:18.879
Read the contract carefully before blank it.

72
00:05:19.179 --> 00:05:22.484
A, sign. B, to sign. C, signing.

73
00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:27.859
The answer is C: signing.

74
00:05:29.017 --> 00:05:32.758
A detailed investigation before a deal is completed is blank.

75
00:05:33.058 --> 00:05:37.141
A, due diligence. B, equity. C, a portfolio.

76
00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.385
The answer is A: due diligence.

77
00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.745
A plan for how investors will sell their stake is an blank.

78
00:05:48.045 --> 00:05:52.337
A, term sheet. B, exit strategy. C, valuation.

79
00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.434
The answer is B: exit strategy.

80
00:05:59.583 --> 00:06:01.420
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:09.368
A, Before to invest, check the team.. B, Before investing, check the team.. C, Before invested, check the team..

82
00:06:12.933 --> 00:06:16.210
The answer is B: Before investing, check the team..

83
00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.799
According to the reading, funds build diversified portfolios because…

84
00:06:22.099 --> 00:06:28.644
A, one big success can repay many losses. B, they dislike risk. C, the law requires it.

85
00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:36.162
The answer is A: one big success can repay many losses.

86
00:06:37.367 --> 00:06:40.425
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:41.283 --> 00:06:45.245
Why might an investor conduct extensive due diligence before investing?

88
00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:03.515
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:03.815 --> 00:07:40.342
An investor conducts extensive due diligence because, once the money is transferred, it is extremely difficult to get back. Before committing funds, the investor needs to confirm that the revenue is real, the contracts are valid and the founders are honest. For instance, hidden debts or exaggerated user numbers could make a promising start-up worthless. It also builds trust, since founders who share information openly are likely to be reliable partners. In short, careful checking protects everyone involved.

90
00:07:40.642 --> 00:07:42.328
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:43.217 --> 00:07:48.307
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and before plus gerund clauses.

92
00:07:48.607 --> 00:07:53.047
Today's value is diligence. Check the details carefully before you commit.

93
00:07:53.347 --> 00:07:55.605
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:55.905 --> 00:07:59.300
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

